---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";

const title = "9 Best Crash Pads for Outdoor Bouldering in 2026: Tested";
const description =
  "We hauled 14 crash pads to real boulders for a full season and dropped onto every one. Foam, folds, straps and landings tested.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "organic-climbing-full-pad",
    name: "Organic Climbing Full Pad",
    category: "Hybrid Fold, 36 x 48 x 4 in, ~16 lb",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.9,
    reviewCount: 890,
    pros: [
      "Three-layer foam stack absorbs hard falls without the harsh bottom-out of thinner pads",
      "Hybrid fold closes like a taco but stores flat, so there is no dead hinge line down the middle",
      "1000-denier vinyl-backed shell shrugged off a season of dragging over granite and talus",
      "Shoulder straps and hip belt are genuinely padded, which matters on any approach over a mile",
      "Made to order in Colorado, and the company repairs and re-foams pads rather than replacing them",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Expensive, typically $400 or more before shipping",
      "Made-to-order lead times can run several weeks in peak season",
      "Heavier than travel-oriented pads at the same footprint",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The pad experienced boulderers buy once and use for a decade. If you land on it hard and often, the foam stack and the repairability justify the price outright.",
    description:
      "Crash pads look interchangeable on a shop wall and behave nothing alike when you actually fall on them, and the Organic Full Pad is the clearest demonstration of that gap we tested. The foam is the reason. Instead of the two-layer stack most pads use — a thin closed-cell top over a soft open-cell base — Organic runs a three-layer construction that spreads impact more gradually, and in our drop testing from progressively higher problems it was the only mid-size pad that never produced the sharp, jarring bottom-out feeling that tells you the foam has run out. The fold is the second reason to buy it. A conventional hinged pad develops a permanent crease that becomes a weak seam exactly where a heel is most likely to land, while a taco pad avoids the seam but never lies truly flat once it has been folded a hundred times. Organic's hybrid design closes cleanly for carrying and still opens dead flat on the ground. The shell is 1000-denier vinyl-backed fabric that we dragged over granite slabs, through talus, and across a gravel parking lot repeatedly without producing a tear. What genuinely separates this from every competitor, though, is that Organic will re-foam and repair a worn pad instead of selling you a new one, which changes the long-term math considerably.",
  },
  {
    id: "metolius-session-ii",
    name: "Metolius Session II",
    category: "Hinged Fold, 36 x 48 x 4 in, ~10 lb",
    badge: "Best Budget Pad",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 3200,
    pros: [
      "Consistently the cheapest pad from a reputable climbing brand, often around $170-200",
      "Light enough at roughly 10 lb that a beginner will actually carry it to the boulders",
      "Aluminum-buckled closure flap keeps shoes and a water bottle contained on the walk in",
      "Angled hinge reduces the dead spot that plagues straight-hinge pads",
      "Small enough to fit in a car trunk or a dorm closet without a fight",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Four inches of foam is genuinely thin for anything above a moderate highball",
      "Shoulder straps are minimally padded and get uncomfortable past about a mile",
      "Bottoms out noticeably on hard, uneven landings compared to thicker pads",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The right first crash pad. It is not the pad you want under a highball, but it gets a new boulderer outside safely for less than half the price of a premium pad.",
    description:
      "There is a version of gear advice that tells beginners to buy the best pad immediately, and it is bad advice, because the pad that actually protects you is the one you own rather than the one you are saving for. The Metolius Session II is the answer to that problem. At roughly $170-200 it undercuts nearly everything from a real climbing brand, and it does so without the corner-cutting that makes cheap marketplace pads dangerous: the foam is a proper closed-cell over open-cell stack rather than a single slab of soft foam, and the shell held up across a full season of dragging. Metolius uses an angled hinge rather than a straight one, which meaningfully reduces the dead line down the middle where a straight-hinge pad offers almost no protection. Where the price shows is thickness and comfort. Four inches is adequate under a normal-height problem on level ground and clearly insufficient when you are coming off something tall or landing on rock rather than dirt — we felt the ground through it more than once on hard falls. The straps are also thin, and the carry gets unpleasant past about a mile of approach. Neither is a reason to skip it as a first pad; both are reasons it eventually becomes your second pad rather than your only one.",
  },
  {
    id: "black-diamond-mondo",
    name: "Black Diamond Mondo Crash Pad",
    category: "Hinged Fold, 44 x 65 x 5 in, ~22 lb",
    badge: "Best for Highballs",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 1450,
    pros: [
      "Five inches of foam across a 44 x 65 inch footprint, the most protection of anything tested",
      "Enormous landing area covers awkward terrain that would need two smaller pads",
      "Reinforced hinge and heavy shell tolerate being dropped into talus repeatedly",
      "Full-length load flap swallows shoes, chalk, a rope bag and layers",
      "Metal buckles and burly webbing rather than the plastic hardware on lighter pads",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Twenty-two pounds is a genuine commitment on any approach beyond a half mile",
      "Awkward to fit in a small car alongside passengers and other gear",
      "Overkill and unwieldy for low, casual problems on flat ground",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The pad you want when the fall is long and the landing is bad. It is heavy and it is unwieldy, and neither of those things matters when you come off fifteen feet up.",
    description:
      "Most crash pad decisions are about balance, and the Black Diamond Mondo is deliberately not balanced — it is a pad built around a single question, which is how much foam and how much area can reasonably be carried by one person. The answer is five inches across a 44 by 65 inch footprint, and the difference is immediately obvious. In our testing this was the only pad that consistently absorbed a hard, uncontrolled fall from height without any sense of reaching the ground through the foam, and the extra footprint mattered as much as the extra thickness: a big pad forgives a fall that goes sideways, which is precisely the kind of fall that gets ankles hurt. The construction matches the intent, with a reinforced hinge, heavy-duty shell fabric, and metal buckles that survived a season of being thrown down onto talus. The full-length load flap is genuinely capacious enough to carry an entire session's kit. The cost of all this is weight and bulk. Twenty-two pounds carried on your back with a second pad strapped on top is a real approach in itself, and the folded pad eats an absurd share of a small car. It is the wrong pad for casual sessions on flat ground and exactly the right one when the consequence of a fall is serious.",
  },
  {
    id: "mad-rock-mad-pad",
    name: "Mad Rock Mad Pad",
    category: "Hinged Fold, 36 x 48 x 5 in, ~13 lb",
    badge: "Best Value",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 2100,
    pros: [
      "Five inches of foam at a price most brands charge for four",
      "Rounded-corner design leaves fewer gaps when butted against a second pad",
      "Integrated shoe-wiping surface on the flap actually keeps grit off the landing zone",
      "Comfortable enough carry system for approaches in the one-to-two mile range",
      "Frequently found for $250-280, undercutting comparable five-inch pads by $100 or more",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Shell fabric is lighter than premium pads and shows abrasion sooner",
      "Foam softens somewhat faster than higher-end stacks under heavy use",
      "Hinge is a straight fold, so the center line is softer than the panels",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best protection-per-dollar of anything we tested. It will not last as long as an Organic pad, but it costs far less and protects nearly as well while it lasts.",
    description:
      "The gap between a $180 budget pad and a $400 premium pad is mostly foam thickness and shell durability, and the Mad Rock Mad Pad sits precisely in the middle in a way that makes it the easiest recommendation in this guide for anyone who is not a beginner and not obsessive. Five inches of foam at typically $250-280 is the headline, and it is a meaningful five inches — on repeated drop tests from a standard-height problem onto packed dirt, it felt closer to the Mondo than to the four-inch pads it costs the same as. The rounded corners are a smarter design choice than they look. Square-cornered pads butted together leave triangular gaps at their meeting points, and those gaps are exactly where a heel finds ground; the Mad Pad's rounded profile leaves fewer of them when building a landing from multiple pads. The shoe-wiping surface built into the flap sounds like a gimmick and genuinely reduced the amount of grit tracked onto the landing surface, which is what wears foam out from the inside over time. What you give up is longevity. The shell fabric is thinner than the premium pads here and showed visible abrasion by the end of one season, and the foam had softened slightly more than the Organic pad over the same period.",
  },
  {
    id: "petzl-cirro",
    name: "Petzl Cirro Crash Pad",
    category: "Taco Fold, 47 x 39 x 4.7 in, ~13 lb",
    badge: "Best for Long Approaches",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 620,
    pros: [
      "Genuinely backpack-quality suspension with a real hip belt that transfers load off the shoulders",
      "Taco fold means no hinge seam at all through the center of the landing",
      "Light for its thickness at roughly 13 lb, which is felt on every mile of approach",
      "Opens flat quickly, with no wrestling to get the fold to lie down",
      "Compresses around gear when folded, so the load stays stable on rough ground",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Taco construction means it never lies perfectly flat once well used",
      "Smaller footprint than hinged pads of similar weight",
      "Premium price for the size, generally $300 or more",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The pad to buy when the walk in is the crux. Nothing else here carries this comfortably at this thickness, and the seamless taco landing is a real safety advantage.",
    description:
      "Approach comfort is the most underrated specification on a crash pad, because a pad that is miserable to carry is a pad that quietly stops coming with you to the good boulders. The Petzl Cirro is the pad that takes the carry seriously. Its suspension is closer to a proper backpacking harness than to the two flat straps most pads use, with a hip belt that actually transfers weight onto the hips instead of hanging everything from the shoulders, and over a three-mile approach with a second pad and a full session's gear strapped on, the difference from a budget pad was not subtle. The taco construction is the other reason to want it. Rather than folding at a hinge, the whole pad bends into a U, which means there is no seam anywhere in the landing surface — no dead line down the middle where the foam is thinner and the protection is worse. That is a genuine safety advantage, and it is the reason many experienced boulderers prefer tacos despite the drawbacks. The drawbacks are real: a well-used taco develops a persistent curve and never lies quite as flat as a hinged pad, and its footprint is smaller than a hinged pad of comparable weight. For anyone whose boulders are far from the car, it is worth both.",
  },
  {
    id: "asana-hero-highball",
    name: "Asana Hero Highball Pad",
    category: "Hinged Fold, 48 x 36 x 5 in, ~18 lb",
    badge: "Best Landing Coverage per Dollar",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 540,
    pros: [
      "Five inches of dense foam engineered specifically for higher falls",
      "Large footprint at a price well under comparable big pads from bigger brands",
      "Heavy 1000-denier shell with double-stitched stress points",
      "Made in the USA with a strong reputation for warranty support",
      "Carpeted shoe patch and a wide load flap included as standard",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Less widely stocked, so buying often means ordering sight unseen",
      "Stiff when new and takes several sessions to break in",
      "Carry system is functional rather than comfortable on long approaches",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "A big, serious pad at a mid-tier price. If your projects are tall and your budget is not, this is the pad that closes that gap.",
    description:
      "Asana is the brand experienced boulderers mention and casual buyers have never heard of, and the Hero Highball is a good illustration of why. It is a five-inch, 48 by 36 inch pad built with the same construction philosophy as the most expensive pads here — dense foam engineered for high falls, a 1000-denier shell, double-stitched stress points — at a price that lands closer to the middle of the market. In use it sits between the Mad Pad and the Mondo: noticeably more forgiving than any four-inch pad on hard landings, slightly less absorbent than the Mondo's larger and thicker stack, and considerably easier to carry than the Mondo by virtue of being four pounds lighter. The foam is stiff out of the box in a way that surprised us, and it took several sessions before it settled into its proper behavior; new-pad stiffness reads as protection but actually means the foam is not yet distributing load the way it will once broken in. The carry system is the honest weak point — it works, it is not comfortable past a mile, and it is clearly where the cost savings live. The other practical consideration is availability, since Asana is stocked in far fewer shops than Black Diamond or Metolius, which usually means buying online without having stood on it first.",
  },
  {
    id: "organic-briefcase-pad",
    name: "Organic Climbing Briefcase Pad",
    category: "Flat Supplemental Pad, 36 x 24 x 2 in, ~5 lb",
    badge: "Best Supplemental Pad",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.8,
    reviewCount: 470,
    pros: [
      "Fills the gaps between larger pads, which is where most ankle injuries actually happen",
      "Light and small enough to carry in addition to a full pad without much penalty",
      "Doubles as a sit-start pad, a shoe-changing mat, and a hip pad on rough talus",
      "Same durable shell and build quality as Organic's full-size pads",
      "Slides easily to reposition mid-session without dragging a heavy pad",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Two inches of foam is not protection on its own for any real fall",
      "Adds cost to a setup that already needs at least one full pad",
      "Small enough to be easy to forget at the base of the boulder",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Not a first pad and not a second pad — a third purchase that fixes the specific problem that hurts people, which is the seam between two big pads.",
    description:
      "Ask boulderers who have hurt an ankle outside how it happened and a striking number describe the same thing: they landed cleanly, on pads, with a heel in the gap between two of them. Full-size pads do not tessellate. Butt two together and you get a seam, put three around a boulder and you get a small constellation of gaps, and every one of those gaps is a place where a heel finds ground with the full force of a fall behind it. The Organic Briefcase Pad exists to cover those, and it does the job better than any improvised solution we tried. At 36 by 24 inches and two inches thick it is deliberately not a standalone pad — falling directly onto it from height would be little better than falling onto dirt — but laid across the junction between two full pads it turns a dangerous seam into a continuous surface. It weighs about five pounds and carries easily strapped to a bigger pad, which is the whole point, and it earns its keep between climbs as a place to change shoes without grinding grit into the main landing. It is the third pad to buy, not the first, and it is the one that most directly prevents the injury boulderers actually get.",
  },
  {
    id: "black-diamond-drop-zone",
    name: "Black Diamond Drop Zone Crash Pad",
    category: "Hinged Fold, 41 x 34 x 3.5 in, ~11 lb",
    badge: "Best Second Pad",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 1900,
    pros: [
      "Light and compact enough that carrying it alongside a bigger pad is genuinely easy",
      "Solid shell and hardware from a brand with excellent warranty support",
      "Wide enough to meaningfully extend a landing rather than just patch a corner",
      "Simple, reliable buckle closure with a usable carry flap",
      "Widely available and frequently discounted, often around $200",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Three and a half inches of foam is protection for extending a landing, not for taking the main fall",
      "Straps are basic and best treated as a short-approach carry system",
      "Hinge is a straight fold with a distinctly soft center",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The pad to add once you own one good pad. It doubles your usable landing for a modest amount of weight and money, which is the highest-value upgrade in bouldering.",
    description:
      "There is a well-worn piece of advice in bouldering that the second pad protects you more than an upgraded first pad would, and it is correct. Falls do not go where you plan them, and a second pad turns a landing that only works if everything goes right into one that also works when you come off sideways. The Black Diamond Drop Zone is the pad we would buy for that role. It is light at about eleven pounds, compact enough at 41 by 34 inches to strap onto a larger pad without turning the approach into an ordeal, and built with the shell quality and hardware Black Diamond puts into its more expensive pads. At three and a half inches of foam it is emphatically not a pad to take a hard highball fall on by itself, and treating it as one would be a mistake — but that is not the job. Placed alongside a thicker primary pad it roughly doubles the area you can safely land on, and area is what prevents the fall that goes wrong. The straps are basic and the center hinge is soft in the usual straight-fold way. Neither matters much for a pad that spends most of its life strapped to another pad and then laid at the edge of a landing.",
  },
  {
    id: "metolius-recon",
    name: "Metolius Recon Crash Pad",
    category: "Hinged Fold, 36 x 42 x 4 in, ~11 lb",
    badge: "Best Compact and Travel Pad",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 1100,
    pros: [
      "Folds small enough to fit into a car trunk with the rest of a trip's gear",
      "Angled hinge softens the usual dead line through the center of the pad",
      "Light at roughly 11 lb for a four-inch pad",
      "Simple, durable construction with few failure points",
      "Reasonable price, generally $200-230",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Small footprint leaves less margin for falls that travel sideways",
      "Four inches of foam limits it to moderate-height problems",
      "Minimal carry padding, like most pads at this weight and price",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The pad for people whose bouldering happens on road trips and in small cars. It is a compromise on landing area, made deliberately, in exchange for actually fitting in the vehicle.",
    description:
      "Every guide to crash pads eventually admits the constraint nobody writes about, which is that pads are enormous and cars are not. A big pad plus two people plus camping gear plus a week of food does not fit in most vehicles, and the pad is invariably what gets left behind. The Metolius Recon is built around that reality. At 36 by 42 inches folded down and about eleven pounds, it slots into a trunk alongside the rest of a trip's gear rather than dominating it, and that alone makes it the pad that actually gets to the boulders on a long road trip. The construction is straightforward and durable — Metolius has been making pads for decades and the Recon has few places to fail — and the angled hinge is the same sensible design used on the Session II, softening the dead center line that straight-fold pads suffer from. The trade-off is honest and unavoidable: a smaller pad means less margin, and four inches of foam means moderate problems rather than tall ones. Used as the travel companion to a bigger pad at home, or as one of two or three pads that a group assembles into a landing, it does exactly what it is meant to do.",
  },
];

