        <div id="how-pads-work" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How a Crash Pad Actually Protects You
          </h2>

          <p>
            A crash pad is not a cushion. That distinction sounds pedantic and it is the single most useful thing to understand about pad construction, because it explains why a four-inch climbing pad protects you and a four-inch foam mattress topper does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every well-built pad uses at least two layers of foam doing two different jobs. The top layer is <strong>closed-cell foam</strong> — firm, dense, and springy, usually around an inch thick. Its job is not to absorb the fall. Its job is to take a concentrated impact, like a heel or a hip landing on a few square inches, and spread it across a much larger area before it reaches the layer below. Without it, all the force of a fall funnels through one point.
          </p>

          <p>
            Underneath sits the <strong>open-cell foam</strong>, typically two to four inches of softer polyurethane. This is the layer that actually absorbs energy, compressing progressively as the now-distributed load pushes into it. Open-cell foam on its own would simply collapse under a heel strike, which is exactly what a mattress does. Closed-cell foam on its own would be firm enough to spread the load and far too rigid to absorb it. It is the pairing that works.
          </p>

          <p>
            This is also why <strong>foam quality matters more than the number printed on the tag</strong>. A five-inch pad built from cheap, low-density open-cell foam will bottom out sooner than a well-engineered four-inch pad, and it will pack out faster over a season of use. It is why the pads in this guide from brands that publish their foam construction consistently outperformed thicker pads from brands that only advertise total thickness.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
