        <!-- SECTION 2: Folds -->
        <div id="folds" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Hinge vs Taco vs Hybrid Folds
          </h2>

          <p>
            How a pad folds determines how it carries, how flat it lies, and — most importantly — whether there is a weak strip running through the middle of your landing. This is the specification most first-time buyers ignore and most experienced boulderers choose on first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hinged Pads</h3>
          <p>
            A hinged pad is two foam panels joined by a fabric hinge, folding shut like a book. They lie perfectly flat, they stack neatly against other pads, and they are the easiest to build a large landing from. The problem is the hinge itself: along that line there is no foam, only fabric, and a heel landing squarely on the hinge gets substantially less protection than one landing on a panel. Some manufacturers angle the hinge diagonally rather than running it straight across, which does not eliminate the weak line but does make it far less likely that both feet land on it at once. That is a meaningful improvement and worth looking for.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Taco Pads</h3>
          <p>
            A taco pad is a single continuous slab of foam that bends into a U shape to be carried. Because the foam is uninterrupted, there is no weak line anywhere in the landing surface — the whole pad protects equally. That is a real safety advantage, and it is why many experienced boulderers will not use anything else as their primary pad. The cost is that foam bent repeatedly develops a memory, so a well-used taco never lies completely flat again and tends to curl slightly at the edges. On uneven ground that curl is barely noticeable; on a flat landing it is mildly annoying.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hybrid Folds</h3>
          <p>
            Hybrid designs use a partial cut or a shaped foam profile so the pad folds cleanly for carrying without a full hinge through the landing surface. Done well, this gets most of the taco's seamless protection with most of the hinged pad's flatness, which is why it is the construction used on several of the more expensive pads here. Done poorly it gets the drawbacks of both. This is a case where brand reputation is a reasonable proxy for whether the design works.
          </p>

