        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Thickness, Footprint, and How Many Pads You Actually Need
          </h2>

          <p>
            The two numbers on a pad's spec sheet answer two different questions, and most buyers over-weight one of them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Thickness Handles Height</h3>
          <p>
            How thick a pad needs to be is a function of how far you are falling and how hard the ground underneath is. Four inches is the practical floor for outdoor use and is genuinely fine for low problems with clean dirt landings. Five inches becomes worth the extra weight when the problems get taller, when the landing is rock rather than soil, or when the climber is on the heavier side — all three concentrate more energy into the foam, and all three make bottoming out more likely.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Footprint Handles Uncertainty</h3>
          <p>
            Area is the specification people underestimate, because it protects against something they are not imagining: the fall that does not go straight down. A controlled drop lands where you expected. A real fall off a hard move sends you backward, sideways, or into a rotation, and the pad that catches that fall is the one covering ground you did not plan to land on. This is why two moderate pads almost always protect better than one excellent pad, even though a single big pad looks like the simpler solution.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Many Pads</h3>
          <p>
            One pad is a starting point and covers low, flat, forgiving problems. Two pads is the honest answer for regular outdoor bouldering — it lets you cover the actual fall zone rather than a guess at it, and it gives you something to overlap seams with. Three or more, usually assembled from what a group brings collectively, is what highballs, traverses, and boulders with sloping or boulder-strewn bases require. Bouldering is a social sport largely because pads are expensive and pooling them is how landings get built.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Never Overlap Pads by Stacking Edges</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              When two pads meet, butt them flush against each other and cover the seam with a small supplemental pad if you have one. Do not overlap one pad's edge on top of another's — the raised lip creates an unstable ridge that rolls an ankle far more effectively than the gap you were trying to close. A flat seam with a small gap is safer than an uneven surface.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Carrying -->
        <div id="carrying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Carrying Comfort and the Approach
          </h2>

          <p>
            The pad you leave in the car protects nobody, and the most common reason a pad stays in the car is that carrying it is miserable. Suspension quality deserves far more weight in a buying decision than it usually gets.
          </p>

          <p>
            The cheapest pads use two flat webbing straps with thin foam padding and nothing else. Under fifteen pounds on a ten-minute approach, that is fine. Under twenty-plus pounds — a big pad, or a pad with a second pad and a session's gear strapped to it — on anything over a mile, it becomes genuinely painful, and the pain lands entirely on your shoulders and trapezius. Better pads add a real hip belt and a sternum strap, which together transfer a large share of the load onto your hips the same way a backpacking pack does. That is the single feature most worth paying for if your boulders are not roadside.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second detail that matters on the approach is the <strong>load flap</strong> — the fabric panel that closes over the open side of a folded pad. A good flap turns the pad into a container that swallows shoes, a chalk bucket, water, and layers, meaning you carry one object instead of a pad plus a pack. A poor flap lets everything migrate and fall out on rough ground. Look for a full-length flap with buckles rather than a token strap.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, consider how the pad rides. A pad that sits high and tight against your back is manageable through talus and scrambling; one that hangs low and swings is a hazard on uneven terrain, where it pulls you off balance exactly when you least want it to. Compression straps that pull the folded pad tight around its contents make a bigger difference here than the spec sheet suggests.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Placement -->
        <div id="placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Pad Placement and Spotting
          </h2>

          <p>
            A good pad badly placed protects less than a mediocre pad placed well, and pad placement is a skill rather than a purchase.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start by identifying the <strong>actual</strong> fall zone rather than the obvious one. Watch where the hard move is, and think about which direction a failure there throws you — a cut foot on an overhang sends you backward and out, while a slip on a slab drops you close to the wall. Place the thickest pad where the worst fall lands, not directly beneath the start.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clear the ground before laying pads down. Rocks, sticks, and roots under a pad transmit through the foam on a hard landing, creating a concentrated pressure point that is exactly what the pad exists to prevent. On sloping ground, use lower pads or packs to level the landing surface before laying the main pad on top, because a landing that tilts will roll an ankle even through good foam.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reposition between attempts. As a session progresses and you get higher on a problem, the fall zone moves, and the pad arrangement that was correct for the first three moves is often wrong for the last two. This is the most common failure we see at the boulders: pads set up once at the start of a session and never moved again.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, a spotter is not there to catch you. A spotter's job is to protect the head and neck and to steer a falling climber toward the pads — hands up, ready at the hips or under the shoulder blades, feet stable, eyes on the climber. Reaching out to catch someone's weight is how spotters get hurt and how climbers get dropped awkwardly. Steer, do not catch.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Care -->
        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Care, Storage, and When to Retire a Pad
          </h2>

          <p>
            Crash pads die in two ways: the shell fails, which is repairable, and the foam packs out, which is mostly not. Everything in caring for a pad is aimed at delaying the second.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Store it flat and unfolded</strong> whenever possible. Foam left compressed in a fold for months develops a permanent set along the crease, which is precisely where you least want it. If a pad has to live folded, unfold it periodically. Store it somewhere dry, too — open-cell foam that gets wet and stays wet grows mold inside the pad where you cannot see or reach it, and a musty pad is often a pad that is already compromised.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Keep grit off the landing surface.</strong> Sand and dirt tracked onto a pad works its way into the foam and abrades it from the inside with every compression. This is why the carpeted shoe patch on the flap of a good pad is a real feature rather than a marketing one. Brush the surface off before folding the pad at the end of a session.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Repair the shell early.</strong> A small tear in the shell fabric grows quickly once dirt and moisture get in. Heavy-duty tape is a legitimate field fix, and most manufacturers will do a proper repair for a fraction of the cost of a new pad.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Retire or re-foam when the pad stops springing back.</strong> The clearest signs a pad is finished are feeling the ground through it on falls that used to feel fine, a visible permanent depression in the middle of the landing area, and foam that stays compressed rather than recovering shape after you stand on it. A regularly used pad reaches that point somewhere between five and ten years, sooner if it has lived outdoors or in a damp garage. Some manufacturers — Organic among them — will replace the foam in an existing shell, which typically costs a fraction of a new pad and is the environmentally and financially better answer.
          </p>

          <p>
            One last note on where a pad lives between trips. A garage is the natural home for a crash pad, and it is also the worst environment for one if the slab is untreated: bare concrete wicks moisture upward continuously, and a pad stored flat on it absorbs that moisture from below. Sealing the floor solves it — our <a href="/best-garage-floor-epoxy/">garage floor epoxy guide</a> covers the coatings that stop slab moisture, and a <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">dehumidifier sized for the space</a> handles the rest in humid climates. Storing the pad on edge against a wall rather than flat on the floor helps regardless.
          </p>
        </div>

