            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best crash pad for outdoor bouldering?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most outdoor boulderers, the Organic Climbing Full Pad offers the best combination of foam quality, seamless landing surface, durability, and long-term repairability. For a first pad on a budget, the Metolius Session II is the most sensible entry point. For tall problems and bad landings, the Black Diamond Mondo provides the most foam and the largest footprint of anything tested.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How thick should a bouldering crash pad be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Four inches is the practical minimum for outdoor bouldering and is adequate for moderate problems on level ground. Five inches is strongly preferable for taller problems, rocky or uneven landings, and heavier climbers. Thickness alone is not the whole story — foam density and layering matter as much, and a well-built four-inch pad can outperform a poorly built five-inch one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many crash pads do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                One good pad is the minimum for low, flat-landing problems. Two pads is the realistic answer for most outdoor bouldering, because falls travel and a single pad rarely covers the whole landing zone. Three or more pads, usually pooled across a group, become necessary for highballs, traverses, and boulders with sloping or rocky bases.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Hinged, taco, or hybrid — which fold is best?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Hinged pads lie flattest and stack most easily, but have a soft dead line down the center where protection is weakest. Taco pads have no seam at all, so the landing surface is uniform, but never lie perfectly flat once broken in. Hybrid folds attempt to combine both and are generally the best option if the budget allows. For a primary landing pad, a taco or hybrid is safer; for a second pad placed alongside others, a hinged pad is more practical.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a crash pad last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A quality pad used regularly lasts roughly five to ten years before the open-cell foam packs out and stops absorbing impact properly. Signs it needs retiring or re-foaming include feeling the ground on ordinary falls, a visible permanent depression in the landing zone, and a pad that no longer springs back after being compressed. Some manufacturers re-foam pads, which is considerably cheaper than replacement.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a mattress or gym mat instead?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Crash pads use a firm closed-cell foam layer over a soft open-cell base specifically so that impact spreads across the surface before compressing. A mattress or a single-layer gym mat compresses straight through under a concentrated heel strike and offers very little real protection from a bouldering fall, while also giving a false sense of security that encourages harder falls.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes When Buying and Using a Crash Pad
          </h2>

          <p>
            Nearly every crash pad problem we have watched play out at the boulders traces back to one of these four, and none of them are about spending more money.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying One Big Pad Instead of Two Moderate Ones</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A single large pad looks like more protection and usually is not, because falls do not land where you plan. Two pads covering a wider area protect against the sideways, backward, and rotating falls that actually hurt people, and they can be repositioned independently as the fall zone moves up a problem. Unless you are specifically working highballs, two mid-size pads beat one big one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Gap Between Pads</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The seam where two pads meet is where a startling share of bouldering ankle injuries happen. Butt the pads flush rather than overlapping their edges, cover the junction with a small supplemental pad when you have one, and check the seam again every time you reposition. An uneven overlapping lip is more dangerous than the gap it was meant to fix.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Setting Pads Once and Never Moving Them</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The fall zone on a boulder problem moves as you get higher, and the arrangement that protected the opening moves is frequently wrong for the crux. Reposition between attempts. It takes fifteen seconds and it is the single highest-value safety habit in outdoor bouldering.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing a Pad Folded in a Damp Garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Foam left compressed takes a permanent set along the fold, and open-cell foam that absorbs moisture from an unsealed concrete slab grows mold inside the pad where you will never see it. Store pads flat or on edge, off the bare floor, somewhere dry. This one habit adds years to a pad's usable life at zero cost.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

